    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d443">

                <pb facs="0572" n="487" sameAs="frus:frus1919v02/pg487"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.51/990</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Assistant Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Assistant Secretary of the Treasury</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Rathbone</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-09-20"><hi rendition="#italic">September 20,
                        1919</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Rathbone</hi>: I am in receipt of your
                    letter of the 12th instant regarding the credit of $5,000,000 established by the
                    Treasury Department in favor of the Government of Liberia and have taken due
                    note of the opinion expressed by you that, as the act authorizing the Secretary
                    of the Treasury to establish credits in favor of foreign governments requires
                    that these credits be established for the national security and defense and to
                    aid in the prosecution of the war, it would seem necessary to limit the advances
                    to Liberia from the credit heretofore established in favor of the Government of
                    that country to the amounts and for the purposes as to which the Government of
                    the United States has already obligated itself.</p>
                <pb facs="0573" n="488" xml:id="pg488"/>
                <p>In reply I beg to inform you that relying on the assurances contained in the
                    letters of October 17 and 25, 1918, from the Treasury Department,<anchor n="33"
                        xml:id="d443-f1"/> the Secretary of State, on behalf of the Government of
                    the United States, has, in fact, entered into obligations to make to Liberia the
                    advances referred to in those letters.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
